def rectangle_intersection_area(r1, r2):
    x_left = max(r1[0], r2[0])
    x_right = min(r1[2], r2[2])
    y_bottom = max(r1[1], r2[1])
    y_top = min(r1[3], r2[3])
    if x_left < x_right and y_bottom < y_top:
        return (x_right - x_left) * (y_top - y_bottom)
    else:
        return 0
```

The function finds the horizontal and vertical overlap for axis-aligned rectangles, each represented as a tuple (x1, y1, x2, y2) with x1 <= x2 and y1 <= y2. The right x-coordinate of the overlap is the smaller of the two right ends, and the left x-coordinate is the larger of the two left ends. The same logic applies to the y-direction; the overlap exists only when both left/right and bottom/top pairs straddle each other, so whenever those pairs do not straddle, the overlap area is zero (which includes touching at edges or points). Each coordinate comparison is done once, and the result is the width of the overlap column times the height of the overlap row. The example rectangles are not referenced anywhere in this code.

