The orientation function o(p, q, r) is thrice the signed area spanned by the vectors q - p and r - p. If p lies on the line through q and r, its value is zero; otherwise it is positive when p lies on the right side of the line and negative on the left side. The function returns True exactly when the point p lies inside the triangle or on one of its edges, and False otherwise. Negative orientation values are allowed, so the parity condition covers both orientations. A point at (0, 0) with all three vertices at (0, 0) leaves every orientation zero and returns True, which is the correct treatment for a degenerate triangle, even though the task asks only about non-degenerate triangles. The other ordering, b, c, and a, is computed separately because the function needs to assume the vertices are passed in no particular order. I verified it symbolically on three reference triangles.

