def point_in_triangle(p, a, b, c):
    def cross(o, a, b):
        return (a[0] - o[0]) * (b[1] - o[1]) - (a[1] - o[1]) * (b[0] - o[0])
    c1 = cross(a, b, p)
    c2 = cross(b, c, p)
    c3 = cross(c, a, p)
    # Non-degenerate triangle: a, b, c are not colinear and form three sides
    # The point lies inside or on an edge of the triangle exactly when all three cross products
    # have the same sign and at least one is zero
    return (c1 >= 0) == (c2 >= 0) == (c3 >= 0) and (c1 == 0 or c2 == 0 or c3 == 0)
```

The central quantity here is the directed area of the triangle formed by each edge and the candidate point, computed as the signed cross product (a.x - o.x)(b.y - o.y) - (a.y - o.y)(b.x - o.x). A point lies to the left of an edge when its contribution is positive and to the right when it is negative, so the point lies inside the triangle exactly when it is on the same side of every edge, meaning all three cross products are either non-negative or non-positive. The stipulation that at least one of them is zero turns this into an inclusion-with-boundary condition, and the equality (c1 >= 0) == (c2 >= 0) == (c3 >= 0) holds exactly when all three are both non-negative or all three are both non-positive. Because the triangle is non-degenerate, no edge includes its two endpoints.

