The function decides whether the point lies inside or exactly on the triangle using the cross product to check which side of each edge the point falls on. For each edge, it computes the cross product of the edge vector with the vector from the edge's starting vertex to the point. A non-negative value means the point lies on the same side of the edge as the triangle's interior or on the edge itself; a negative value means it lies on the opposite side and outside the triangle. The point inside or on an edge of the triangle gives non-negative results for all three edges, and the point outside gives at least one negative result. The comparisons use >=, so an edge point with a cross product of exactly zero is counted as lying inside. If two triangle vertices coincide, the sign test still places the shared point on all edges, and the result is True, as with the case where a, b and c are (0, 0), (4, 0) and (0, 4) and p is (0, 0).

