import math

def classify_triangle_by_sides(p1, p2, p3):
    def distance(p, q):
        return math.hypot(p[0] - q[0], p[1] - q[1])
    d1 = distance(p1, p2)
    d2 = distance(p2, p3)
    d3 = distance(p3, p1)
    lengths = {d1, d2, d3}
    if len(lengths) == 1:
        return 'equilateral'
    elif len(lengths) == 2:
        return 'isosceles'
    else:
        return 'scalene'
```

The function calculates the squared distances between each pair of vertices and reads them off the set of distinct distances. A triangle is equilateral when all three distances are equal, isosceles when exactly two are equal, and scalene when all three differ. Because floating-point comparisons on squared lengths are exact and the triangle is guaranteed to be non-degenerate, the set size test gives the right classification without requiring a threshold or tolerance.

