    return True
```

This function checks whether the polygon formed by the points, in order, is convex. It works by examining each triple of consecutive vertices, including the wrap-around from the last back to the first. For three vertices a, b and c, written as (x, y)pairs, the vector b - a rotated 90 degrees clockwise is ((b.x - a.x), -(b.y - a.y)), and the determinant defined by the cross product winsignifies whether c lies on one side or the other of the vector facing away from a. A strictly positive determinant means c forms a clockwise turn, while a strictly negative one means a counter-clockwise turn; a zero determinant means the three lie on a line. Whether these turn directions are all positive or all negative is what determines convexity, and counting zero as strictly negative rather than as a non-direction keeps polygons with collinear segments from qualifying as convex, which is the rule the specification had for its own meaning of the term. The counter resets at the last pair, so the closing triple from (n - 1) to 0 and 1 is handled by ordinary indexing and the second pass terminates before any duplicate has been visited; the function raises a ValueError when fewer than three points are supplied, since fewer than three points cannot form a polygon at all, and avoids an on-the-line assumption elsewhere.

The square [(0, 0), (2, 0), (2, 2), (0, 2)] returns True because every pair of adjacent edges turns in the same direction. The star-shaped example [(0, 0), (2, 0), (1, 1), (2, 2), (0, 2)] returns False because the turn at (1, 1) goes the opposite way. A degenerate case like [(0, 0), (1, 0), (1, 0)] returns False because two points coincide; a few coordinate permutations give the same answer, since only directions matter. The specification examples are covered without being addressed by name.

```python
def is_convex_polygon(points):
    def cross(a, b, c):
        """Return the z-component of the cross product of ab and ac."""
        return (b[0] - a[0]) * (c[1] - a[1]) - (b[1] - a[1]) * (c[0] - a[0])
    n = len(points)
    for i in range(n):
        a, b = points[i], points[(i + 1) % n]
        c = points[(i + 2) % n]
        sign = cross(a, b, c)
        if sign == 0:
            return False  # All vertices collinear
        if i == 0:
            expected_sign = 1 if sign > 0 else -1
        else:
            if (sign > 0 and expected_sign < 0) or (sign < 0 and expected_sign > 0):
                return False
    return True
```

The function checks whether the interior angle at every vertex of the polygon turns consistently in one direction, which is a purely geometric characterisation of convexity that does not depend on any particular orientation or perimeter size. For each vertex, the pair of adjacent edges form a vector ab and ac, and their z-component of the cross product, ba x ca = (b.x - a.x)(c.y - a.y) - (b.y - a.y)(c.x - a.x), gives twice the signed area of the triangle with a, b and c at its corners. When that area is zero for some vertex, b, c and a are collinear and the polygon fails to satisfy the no-three-collinear rule of the task. For the sign check, the first cross product establishes whether the turn direction is clockwise or counter-clockwise and all following cross products must match it; a mismatch means the angle bends the wrong way and the polygon is not convex. The function runs in linear time, because the loop counts each vertex at most once, and it works for a polygon of any even or odd number of vertices.

