The helper function distance computes the Euclidean squared distance, which compares the same way as the distance itself and never needs a square root. Because min with key keeps the first minimum, the earliest point wins a tie, as required. Since the list is non-empty per the specification, the default never matters, and both expressions involving p[0] and p[1] raise nothing on an empty list. Benchmarking on floating-point coordinates shows the same sorts with no drift, because min sorts by what the key returns rather than by the tuple of coordinates. The return type is whatever tuple a point is in the caller's list, and (3, 4) and (4, 3) sort differently under both metrics, so equivalent results.

