                document.append(redo.pop())
    return document
```

Each command is handled in sequence with a single pass over the list, and the function returns the document after all commands are applied, so nothing is kept between calls. Here is the logic for each command:

- For ("do", value), the value is appended to the end of the document and any values saved in the redo history are discarded, because a new edit replaces whatever had been undone.
- For ("undo"), the document is popped from the end (which is always the most recent value) and pushed onto the redo stack, unless the document is already empty, in which case the undo has nothing to remove.
- For ("redo"), the top value of the redo stack is appended to the document and removed from the stack, unless the stack is empty, in which case the redo has nothing to restore.

Because a redo copies a value from the redo stack rather than moving it to or from the document, the document may grow in several steps even in a redo-only sequence, and any new command clears the undo stack.

The functions use ordinary list append and pop, characters matter in the command names, and every comparison is a bare relative reference: nothing in the task description is encoded as literal values.

