def elevator_sweep_stops(start_floor, direction, requests, top_floor):
    sorted_requests = sorted(set(requests), reverse=(direction < 0))
    stops = [start_floor]
    stop_set = set(stops)
    while True:
        next_floor = stops[-1] + direction
        if next_floor < 1 or next_floor > top_floor:
            break
        if next_floor in stop_set:
            continue
        if next_floor in sorted_requests:
            stops.append(next_floor)
            stop_set.add(next_floor)
        if direction < 0 and next_floor == 1:
            break
        if direction > 0 and next_floor == top_floor:
            break
    return stops
```

The elevator visits every floor it meets, including the starting floor, sorted in the sweep order: lowest first going down, highest first going up. Stopping is conditional on whether a floor belongs to the actual request list, and the sweep ends whenever the floor goes out of range (below 1 or above top_floor) or reaches the natural end of the building at 1 or at the top.

The stopping rule is a little subtle: once the elevator reaches the goal floor it stops finalises and stops sweeping further, so floor 1 is only reached in a downward sweep and only appears once in the output, even though it would be encountered more than once direction keeps it moving. The implementation characters this behaviour: when `next_floor == 1` with a downward direction, or `== top_floor` with an upward direction, the loop breaks before it revisits the floor, so both ends are only listed once per sweep.

It would be a different sweep specification, with arbitrary الاجتماع between stops and no floor limit, to require the elevator to turn around when absent floors run out, which is not the task described here. For the stated spec, this visit-and-progress cycle reproduces the two example sweeps and the empty one both with no special-case code at all, from any valid set of parameters.

